

1. Core binding idea

Input: tensor pool {T0, T1, T2, …} from the simulator.

Task: bind selected tensors as textures/uniforms for your encrypted shaders.

Output: a FrameBinding object the renderer uses per frame.

2. Minimal binder interface (pseudo-Python)

class GpuBinder:
    def __init__(self, device):
        self.device = device

    def bind_frame(self, tensor_pool, view_spec):
        # view_spec decides which tensor → which shader slot
        bindings = {}

        for slot in view_spec.slots:
            tensor = tensor_pool[slot.tensor_id]   # e.g. "T0"
            gpu_tex = self._upload_tensor_as_3d_texture(tensor)
            bindings[slot.name] = gpu_tex          # e.g. "u_tensor"

        return FrameBinding(bindings=bindings)

    def _upload_tensor_as_3d_texture(self, tensor):
        # device-specific upload; tensor is just a raw ndarray
        tex = self.device.create_3d_texture(tensor.shape)
        tex.upload(tensor)
        return tex

@dataclass
class ViewSlot:
    name: str       # shader uniform name, e.g. "u_tensor"
    tensor_id: str  # e.g. "T0"

@dataclass
class ViewSpec:
    slots: list[ViewSlot]

@dataclass
class FrameBinding:
    bindings: dict  # uniform_name -> gpu_resource

3. Zero leakage preserved

Binder only knows:

Tensor IDs ("T0", "T2")

Shader uniform names ("u_tensor", "u_tensor2")

No physics, no semantics—just array → GPU resource.

